-- rules/spec-coverage.rofl — coverage of the SPECIFICATION, not of the code.
--
-- The kernel's own obligations, modelled so that the engine whose behaviour
-- they govern can be asked about them. The question no grep and no coverage
-- tool in this repository can answer today:
--
--     which obligation has no check, and which test guards nothing anyone
--     wrote down.
--
-- A DUTY is one sentence in one document. It carries its citation in the same
-- fact, so a duty with no provenance is not a thing this model can represent
-- — the fabrication `examples/yak` is about is excluded by the shape rather
-- than caught by an audit. What the audit catches is the citation that no
-- longer resolves (`unfounded`), which is the failure mode that survives.
--
-- EDB — one ledger per source document (facts/spec.rofl):
--   duty[Doc](Id, Kind, File, Line, Anchor)
--       Kind: guaranteed | prohibited | limit | acceptance | done
--       Anchor: text that must occur at File:Line, verified by scanners/spec.ts
--
-- EDB — [map], the librarian's editorial links (facts/spec.rofl):
--   cited_check(C, File, Name)   a check named as it is written in the tree
--   guards(C, Duty)              this check is what stands behind that duty
--   amends(New, Old)             a later decision replaces an earlier sentence
--   qualifies(New, Old)          a later sentence narrows one without retiring it
--   restates(New, Old)           the same obligation, written twice
--   discharged_by(Duty, Path)    an obligation whose discharge is an artefact,
--                                not a test (a document, a paragraph)
--
-- EDB — [checks], written by scanners/spec.ts (`authority(checks, census)`):
--   check(File, Name, Kind)      Kind: test | gate | ci
--   test_file(File)              every test/*.test.ts in the tree
--   citation_ok(Duty)            the anchor was found at the cited line
--   artifact_present(Duty)       the artefact named by discharged_by exists
--
-- All eleven are declared extensional below: every one of them can legitimately
-- be EMPTY (a census that found nothing, a run with no broken citation), and
-- `undefined_premise[audit]` reads emptiness as a misspelling unless the input
-- is declared. Declaring the input is the remedy, not a waiver — the same
-- discipline `boot.rofl` applies to `imports`.

edb(duty).
edb(cited_check).
edb(guards).
edb(amends).
edb(qualifies).
edb(restates).
edb(discharged_by).
edb(check).
edb(test_file).
edb(citation_ok).
edb(artifact_present).

-- The working relation, gathered out of the document ledgers with the ledger
-- kept as an ordinary argument. The bracket on the head IS the declaration of
-- the crossing (`test/persp-var.test.ts`), so this is a bridge and not a leak.
duty_of[coverage](O, K, P, F, L, A) :- duty[P](O, K, F, L, A).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- change ---

-- An obligation a later decision replaced. It stays in the model — the
-- document still says it — but it is not what the kernel owes today, so it
-- is not counted against coverage.
superseded[coverage](Old) :- amends[map](_, Old).

-- ...and one that a later sentence NARROWED without withdrawing. The
-- distinction is load-bearing: a qualified obligation is still owed, so it
-- still has to be covered. Recording `Facts may be asserted into reserved
-- relations` as an amendment would have retired the rule-rejection duty that
-- `test/phase3.test.ts` still holds — the model lying in the direction that
-- flatters it.
qualified[coverage](Old, New) :- qualifies[map](New, Old).
live_duty[coverage](O)    :- duty_of[coverage](O, _, _, _, _, _),
                             not superseded[coverage](O).

-- ------------------------------------------------------------- coverage ---

-- A cited check STANDS when the census found it under that exact name.
check_stands[coverage](C)  :- cited_check[map](C, F, N), check[checks](F, N, _).

-- ...and it DANGLES when it did not: the test was renamed or deleted, and the
-- citation now names nothing. This is the rule that makes the model an audit
-- rather than a bibliography — deleting a guard test moves a duty from
-- covered to uncovered without anyone editing this file.
dangling[coverage](C, O)   :- guards[map](C, O), cited_check[map](C, F, N),
                              not check[checks](F, N, _).

-- ...and a guard naming a check id that nothing declares at all. Without
-- this rule such a link is INVISIBLE: `guarded_by` needs a `cited_check` to
-- join through, so a typo'd id silently guards nothing and its duty reports
-- as uncovered with no explanation. An audit whose failure mode is silence
-- is the failure mode this repository exists to remove.
undefined_citation[coverage](C, O) :- guards[map](C, O), not cited_check[map](C, _, _).

-- What kind of instrument stands behind an obligation: a test, a gate script,
-- a CI step. The distinction the documents cannot make about themselves.
guarded_by[coverage](O, K) :- guards[map](C, O), cited_check[map](C, F, N),
                              check[checks](F, N, K).
covered[coverage](O)       :- guarded_by[coverage](O, _).

-- THE ANSWER. An obligation the kernel owes today with nothing mechanical
-- behind it: it holds because the prose says so.
uncovered[coverage](O)     :- live_duty[coverage](O), not covered[coverage](O).

-- Not every uncovered duty is unattended. `document the omission` is
-- discharged by a document; the model says which document, and the census
-- says whether it is still there.
prose_discharged[coverage](O) :- uncovered[coverage](O), artifact_present[checks](O).
unattended[coverage](O)       :- uncovered[coverage](O),
                                 not prose_discharged[coverage](O).

-- The sharp end: a PROHIBITION with no mechanical check. Nothing at all
-- stands between the next author and the shortcut.
open_shortcut[coverage](O) :- uncovered[coverage](O),
                              duty_of[coverage](O, prohibited, _, _, _, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------- fabrication ---

-- A duty whose anchor is not at the line it names. Either the document moved
-- under the model, or the sentence was never there — and the model cannot
-- tell those apart, which is why it reports the citation and not a verdict.
unfounded[coverage](O)     :- duty_of[coverage](O, _, _, _, _, _),
                              not citation_ok[checks](O).

-- --------------------------------------------------------- the converse ---

-- A test file no duty cites: behaviour the tree guards and no document in the
-- model demands. Reported per FILE, because a single test is guarded prose at
-- the wrong granularity — obligations are sentences, and one sentence is
-- normally guarded by one or two named tests out of a file of twenty.
named_file[coverage](F)    :- guards[map](C, _), cited_check[map](C, F, _).
unwritten[coverage](F)     :- test_file[checks](F), not named_file[coverage](F).

-- ============ what [coverage] is allowed to read ============
--
-- Written out because the kernel stopped writing it. `crossing` used to carry
-- `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)`, emitted for any rule whose head named a ledger
-- and whose body read another, so every rule above licensed its own read by
-- existing and this file reported `leak[audit]` 0 for a reason unrelated to
-- the file. It reports three crossings now, and they are declared here.
--
-- [coverage] READS [map], the citation book: `guards`, `cited_check` and
-- `amends`/`qualifies` are what a coverage verdict is COMPUTED FROM. Reading
-- them is the whole of this model.
imports(coverage, map).

-- [coverage] READS [checks], the book of tests that exist on disk. Same
-- reason: `check_stands` and `unwritten` compare a citation against the file
-- it names, and there is no way to do that without reading both books.
imports(coverage, checks).

-- AND the gather `imports` cannot express. `duty_of[coverage](O, K, P, F, L,
-- A) :- duty[P](O, K, F, L, A).` reads a VARIABLE ledger: seven documents each
-- keep their duties in their own book ([boot], [decided], [limits], [perf],
-- [readme], [spec], [wfs]), and the point of the relation is that it does not
-- care which. `$var("P")` has no `authority` fact and can never be the From of
-- an import, so `collects` is the only sentence available — and it is true:
-- this ledger deliberately gathers from books it does not name.
--
-- Narrow: `collects_from` requires `not perspective(A)`, so the two named
-- crossings above still need the two `imports` lines above, and would still be
-- reported without them.
collects(coverage).
